@@s001 Neural networks: gradient descent and backpropagation
@@s002 dato:L(w) = (w − 3)²,ok:η = 0.1,ora:η = 0.9,no:η = 1.1
@@s003 The loss L(w) = (w − 3)², minimum at w = 3. Start from w = 0. Rule: w ← w − η · 2(w − 3).
@@s004 <b>η = 0.1</b>, first step: the gradient at 0 is −6, so w = 0 − 0.1 · (−6) = <b>0.6</b>.
@@s005 Following steps: 1.08, 1.464, 1.771, 2.017… It approaches 3 with ever shorter steps, because the gradient shrinks.
@@s006 <b>η = 0.9</b>: it overshoots the minimum at each step (0 → 5.4 → 1.08 → 4.54 → 1.77 → 3.98) but the oscillations narrow: it converges all the same.
@@s007 <b>η = 1.1</b>: every step overshoots more (0 → 6.6 → −1.32 → 8.18 → −3.22 → 10.47): the loss grows, it <b>diverges</b>. For this loss the limit is η &lt; 1.
@@s008 dato:input,ora:neuron computing,ok:computed value
@@s009 The network 2 → 2 → 1 of the text. On the edges the weights W₁ = [[0.5, −1], [1, 0.5]] and W₂ = [1, −2]; biases b₁ = [0, 1], b₂ = 0.5.
@@s010 Input x = [1, 2].
@@s011 h1: z = 0.5·1 + (−1)·2 + 0 = <b>−1.5</b>. ReLU(−1.5) = <b>0</b>: the neuron "switches off".
@@s012 h2: z = 1·1 + 0.5·2 + 1 = <b>3</b>. ReLU(3) = <b>3</b>.
@@s013 Output: z = 1·0 + (−2)·3 + 0.5 = <b>−5.5</b>; with the sigmoid p = σ(−5.5) ≈ <b>0.0041</b>: a probability of class 1 almost zero.
@@s014 dato:input,ok:forward values,ora:backward gradients
@@s015 The graph of DW 26: f(x, y, z) = (x + y) · z. A "+" node computes q = x + y, a "×" node computes f = q · z.
@@s016 <b>Forward</b>: x = 1, y = 2, z = 3.
@@s017 q = 1 + 2 = <b>3</b>.
@@s018 f = 3 · 3 = <b>9</b>. The forward pass remembers the intermediate values: they are needed to go back.
@@s019 <b>Backward</b>: one starts from the output with ∂f/∂f = 1.
@@s020 "×" node: the derivative with respect to one input is the other input. ∂f/∂q = z = <b>3</b>, ∂f/∂z = q = <b>3</b>.
@@s021 "+" node: passes the gradient as it is (local derivative 1). Chain rule: ∂f/∂x = ∂f/∂q · 1 = <b>3</b>, ∂f/∂y = <b>3</b>.
@@s022 dato:28 × 28 image,ora:connections / filter,ok:parameters
@@s023 no:linear (identity),dato:sigmoid,due:tanh,ok:ReLU
@@s024 Neural networks: gradient descent and backpropagation
@@s025 Lectures 13–14 and the exercises of week 14 (network for the gaze, optimisation, derivatives and the chain rule). A network is a chain of inner products and activation functions; it is trained by going down the gradient of the loss, computed with the chain rule. Exam question <b>Q12</b>. DW chapters 25–26.
@@s026 Gradient descent
@@s027 The idea
@@s028 The <a href="w08-derivate-bordi.html">gradient</a> points where the loss rises most: one takes a step in the opposite direction. η is the <b>learning rate</b> (the length of the step). One repeats until the gradient is almost 0 or for a fixed number of steps.
@@s029 Each step approaches 3, with ever shorter steps because the gradient shrinks near the minimum.
@@s030 For linear least squares it is enough to solve a system; with sigmoids and networks (non-linear in the weights) not.
@@s031 Three learning rates on the same loss
@@s032 All the points computed with Python.
@@s033 The example of DW 25: f(x₁, x₂) = 0.5x₁² + 2.5x₂² − x₁x₂ − 2(x₁ + x₂)
@@s034 Verified: the gradient at (3, 1) is [0, 0].
@@s035 After 50 steps (2.94, 0.99): it gets there, slowly.
@@s036 Learning rate too small: slow. Too large: the steps overshoot the minimum and grow. Here the limit is 2/5.24 ≈ 0.38 (5.24 is the largest eigenvalue of the matrix of second derivatives, the Hessian).
@@s037 Convexity
@@s038 A convex function (bowl-shaped, positive second derivative) has a single minimum, and gradient descent finds it. The loss of a network is not convex: one can end in a local minimum, and the starting point matters.
@@s039 The network: forward pass
@@s040 A network 2 → 2 → 1 (ReLU, then sigmoid)
@@s041 Each neuron of the hidden layer computes an inner product weights · input plus a bias: all together is a matrix times a vector (<a href="nozioni-dimensioni.html">(2 × 2)(2) → 2</a>).
@@s042 The non-linear activation. Without it, two linear layers in a row would still be a single linear transformation.
@@s043 Output for binary classification: a probability (sigmoid). For regression the output remains z₂ (identity activation) and the loss is the MSE. Numbers verified with NumPy.
@@s044 The values flowing forwards
@@s045 Activation functions: why they are needed
@@s046 Each neuron does two things: an inner product <code>z = wᵀx</code> (as in linear regression) and then an <b>activation function</b> <code>g(z)</code>. Without activation, a network with many layers would be useless: two linear layers <code>W₂(W₁x)</code> are the same as a single matrix <code>(W₂W₁)x</code>, so the whole network would remain a linear model.
@@s047 The activations of the course: the <b>sigmoid</b> σ(z) = 1/(1 + e⁻ᶻ), between 0 and 1, which is read as a probability (logistic regression, output for two classes); the <b>tanh</b>, same shape between −1 and 1; the <b>ReLU</b> max(0, z), the standard choice in the hidden layers, because it is simple and its derivative does not switch off for large z (that of the sigmoid does, and training slows down). On the output the activation is chosen according to the task: identity for regression (the gaze network predicts two numbers), sigmoid or softmax for classification.
@@s048 With a non-linearity between the layers, combining neurons produces new functions: for example <code>|z| = ReLU(z) + ReLU(−z)</code>, a V that no linear model can represent. A neural network is therefore a <b>non-linear prediction function</b>: a composition of linear transformations and non-linearities, whose weights are learned by gradient descent.
@@s049 Sigmoid, tanh, ReLU, and why they are needed
@@s050 Backpropagation = chain rule
@@s051 The graph of DW 26: f(x, y, z) = (x + y) · z at (1, 2, 3)
@@s052 The intermediate values are computed and remembered.
@@s053 Local derivatives of the "times" node: each is the value of the other input.
@@s054 The "plus" node passes the gradient backwards as it is (local derivative 1). Chain rule: the derivatives are multiplied along the path (<a href="nozioni-derivate.html">rules</a>).
@@s055 Forward the values, backward the gradients
@@s056 A neuron with sigmoid and log loss
@@s057 The <a href="w10-classificatore-lineare.html">logistic regression</a> is a network with a single neuron.
@@s058 Three factors of the chain; with σ′ = σ(1 − σ) almost everything simplifies. With w = [0.3, −0.2], x = [1, 2], y = 1: gradient [−0.525, −1.050], equal to the numerical one.
@@s059 Each layer receives the gradient from the next one, multiplies it by its local derivative and passes it to the previous one. Then each weight takes a step of gradient descent.
@@s060 MLP and CNN, training and evaluating
@@s061 MLP (fully connected)
@@s062 CNN (convolutional)
@@s063 layer
@@s064 every output connected to <b>all</b> the inputs
@@s065 a small <a href="w07-filtri.html">filter</a> slides over the image
@@s066 parameters, image 28 × 28
@@s067 784 → 100 neurons: <b>78 500</b>
@@s068 16 filters 3 × 3: <b>160</b>
@@s069 idea
@@s070 no assumption on the structure
@@s071 local neighbourhood and the same weights in every position (weight sharing): the filters it learns resemble those for edges and HOG
@@s072 <b>Loss</b>: MSE for regression (for example the gaze network, which predicts (x', y')), log loss / cross-entropy for classification.
@@s073 <b>Training</b>: epochs of gradient descent, often on small groups of data (mini-batches). Learning rate to be chosen.
@@s074 <b>Overfitting</b>: many parameters ⇒ training and validation curves that separate (<a href="w07-complessita-modello.html">week 7</a>). Remedies: more data, early stopping, regularisation (<a href="w07-validazione.html">ridge = weight decay</a>), smaller networks. Choices of architecture and hyperparameters with a validation set or cross-validation.
@@s075 Q12
@@s076 (a) prediction for regression and classification (output and activation). (b) training: gradients, chain rule, forward and backward (the graph of DW). (c) training and evaluation, over/underfitting. (d) MLP against CNN. (e) loss, complexity, cross-validation.
@@s077 78 500 parameters against 160
